  That's the spirit.  Once you realize how to play a character properly
it's like a whole new game.  Early jumping boots become as welcome than
speed boots.  (Be wary of the nutrition cost of jumping, especially
early in the game.)  GoP are early game breakers when combined with
Expert thrown daggers.  Or Expert darts.  Even rocks become deadly fired
one at a time.  NB: Missiles fired from launchers do NOT get the
strength damage bonus.

  Wizards, with their starting cloak of magic resistance, are probably
the most fun early characters to play, if you're not addicted to "Run up
and HIT something."

  Part of that fun is that how you play matters.  "Run up and hit
something" will just get you killed.  (Whereas you might luck out and
survive with a combat wombat.)  Another part is that you have to work a
little harder.  Rogues survive by throwing daggers early and often as
well.  But Rogues start out with a stack of daggers and basic skill in
dagger.  Wizards have to find their daggers and build up skill from
scratch.  It's satisfying when you're finally firing up to three daggers
at a time from a stack of 6 or so.

  So for your next roles after Wizard try the other classes that can get
to Expert dagger: Ranger, Rogue, Tourist and Valkyrie.  

  Note Rogues and Rangers get a chance of an additional dagger thrown
per fling.  Thats up to 4 daggers at Expert skill, averaging to 2.5x
daggers.

  Rogues can also get a backstab bonus of (XL/2) per hit on a scared
monster.  Scare monsters with the spell of scare monster, tooled horn,
leather drum, drum of earthquake, scroll of scare monster, and Elbereth.
A Rogue with Magicbane is in great shape.  Note that at XL:10 the
backstab bonus is 5, multiplied by 2.5 per fling is 12.5 damage bonus
right there.  At XL:30?  15 * 2.5 = 37 additional damage.

  So a Rogue that does Elbereth and throws daggers early and often is
powerful indeed.  What's the catch?  Wait until you get to the Quest.
Heh heh heh.

  Valkyries are the combat wombats of death with Expert thrown daggers.
That's why the DevTeam put in the #twoweapon Deathtrap for Valks.
#twoweapon takes 2-3 times the skill slots, twice as many b?oEW, uses a
weaker skill damage table, and is pitifully limited in range.  (It's not
so much that NetHack tries to kill your character as it gleefully lets
you kill your character.)

